==Latin==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|ine-pro|la}} {{recons|kauput-|lang=ine-pro}}, {{recons|kaput|káput|head|lang=ine-pro}}. [[cognate|Cognates]] include {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Haupt|lang=de}} and English {{term|head|lang=en}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|Classical}} {{IPA|/ˈka.put/|lang=la}}, {{X-SAMPA|/"ka.put/|lang=la}}

===Noun===
{{la-noun|caput|capitis|capitis|n|third}}

# [[head]]
# {{New Latin|anatomy|lang=la}} headlike [[protuberance]] on an [[organ]] or [[body]] part, usually [[bone]], for instance ''caput [[ulna|ulnae]]''
# {{New Latin|medicine|lang=la}} a [[disease]]; a severe swelling of the soft tissues of a newborn's scalp that develops as the baby travels through the birth canal.
# {{figuratively}} The [[vital]] part
# {{context|lang=la|of a river}} [[origin]], [[source]], [[head]]
#* [[w:en:Quintus Horatus Flaccus|Horace]], ''Carmina'', 1:1
#*: ''nunc ad aquae lene '''caput''' sacrae.''
# {{figuratively|lang=la}} [[life]]
# [[capital city]]
#* [[w:Livy|Titus Livius]], ''Ab Urbe Condita'', 1:16
#*: ''ut mea Roma '''caput''' orbis terrarum sit''
#* [[w:en:Ovid|Ovid]], ''Ab Urbe Condita'', 8:4
#*: ''Romam '''caput''' Latio esse''
# {{context|poetic|lang=la}} [[leader]], [[chief]]
# {{context|lang=la|in writings}} {{l/en|division}}, {{l/en|section}}, {{l/en|paragraph}}, {{l/en|chapter}}
# {{inflection of|lang=la|caput|caput|acc|s}}
# {{inflection of|lang=la|caput|caput|voc|s}}

====Usage notes====
{{term||lang=la|Caput}} can be used with either a genitive or a dative in the sense of a [[capital city]].

====Inflection====
{{la-decl-3rd-N|caput|caput|capit}}

